# 0040 - a group's relays stay awake, and a peer the first handshake attempt does not
#        reach is looked for on this client's own relays as well.
#
# S1-AA, re-opened 2026-09-19 by its own census: patch 0029 booked its falsifier -- "a reap
# with attempts 8-10, last packet 30 s ago ... would mean the target's relay path is the
# whole story and the timeout was never the lever" -- and that evening it came, in two of
# six runs of the 8+1 rig. runs/split201156-9, read to the line:
#
#   * The far seat (relay-only) shook hands with the founder at 13.8 s, and its sync over
#     relays was slow that evening: it read "group 0 seen" at 32 s and the founder on the
#     group's line only at 42.5 s, so the eight seats here were told of it between 38 and
#     84 s, not at 15 to 20 s as on a good evening.
#   * By then every seat here had put its relays to sleep (27 to 38 s): their peers were
#     all direct, and a relay sleeps when every connection that locks it is asleep. That
#     is a messenger's economy. In a group it closes the one door a relay-only member can
#     knock at: a member is announced by ONE relay of its own (GCA_MAX_ANNOUNCED_TCP_RELAYS
#     is 1 and that is the wire's), the request goes there out of band, and a relay
#     delivers out of band only to a client that is CONNECTED to it.
#   * And the other direction hangs on one relay too, drawn at random by whoever answers
#     the sync (create_sync_announce). One seat could not connect to the relay it was
#     given for the far seat -- ten seconds, slot killed -- and all ten attempts of the
#     entry's thirty seconds read "no TCP relay carried it either: 0 online, 0 registered",
#     while both ends held half a dozen healthy relays of the same short public list.
#   * Four of the eight never shook hands with the far seat in 120 s; five reaped it. The
#     founder gave the far seat's place away (D-060: it could not hear two seats for 20 s),
#     and the table was played by eight. Where the far seat was known BEFORE the relays
#     slept -- most runs -- nothing was reaped.
#
# The change, local, nothing on the wire:
#   (a) TCP_connection.c: an instance can be told its relays stay awake
#       (p2p_poker_tcp_relays_stay_awake), and do_tcp_conns puts none of them to sleep;
#       group_chats.c says so for every group's own instance (init_gc_tcp_connection).
#       The main instance, net_crypto's, is untouched.
#   (b) group_chats.c, send_gc_handshake_packet: a peer that has not shaken hands by the
#       second attempt is registered on up to four of this client's own connected relays
#       (p2p_poker_register_on_own_relays; two of a connection's six slots stay free for
#       the relays the peer shares once confirmed). Once per entry, and said at DEBUG.
#       Both ends run it, so a relay they share carries the request out of band at once
#       and reads ONLINE as soon as both have asked.
#
# THE PREDICTION IS BOOKED BEFORE THE RUN, plain 8+1 runs read with
# tools/read-join-phase.py:
#   1. no seat reaps the far seat's entry in the join phase, and the far seat reaps nobody;
#   2. every seat here that is told of the far seat shakes hands with it within fifteen
#      seconds of being told, whenever that is;
#   3. the far seat is never given back for not hearing the table (D-060's MESH_GRACE).
# THE FALSIFIER: a reap of "attempts 10" on an entry for which (b)'s line was printed with
# one or more relays added. That would mean the two ends share no relay, or that a
# request a relay carries out of band is lost further on -- and that neither sleeping
# relays nor the single announced relay is the lever.
#
# THE RUNS (2026-09-19, the 8+1 rig, read with tools/read-far-seat-join.py).
#
# A plain run (runs/split211403-9) held, and so did one with the far seat started sixty
# seconds late (-FarDelay 60, runs/split212307-9) -- and so did the SAME late start WITHOUT
# this patch (runs/split213510-9): seats whose relays had slept seven to nine times shook
# hands with the late far seat in two to five seconds. **So a late arrival onto sleeping
# relays is not, by itself, the failure, and the first reading above claimed more than it
# had.** What the bad evening run had besides was a relay that could not be connected to.
#
# That is reproduced on demand by a knob this patch adds under P2P_POKER_FAULT_HARNESS:
# P2P_POKER_DEAD_ANNOUNCED_RELAY=1 makes the one relay a relay-only member is announced
# by an address nothing routes under a key no relay holds. A second, P2P_POKER_NO_0040=1,
# switches this patch off, so that a control and its treatment are ONE binary
# (tools/table-run-split.ps1 -DeadAnnouncedRelay, -NoPatch0040, -FarDelay). Both ends
# blind, back to back:
#
#   without 0040 (runs/split214815-9)        with 0040 (runs/split215607-9)
#   the far seat played 0 hands              24 hands opened by nine seats
#   2 of 7 seats never in contact with it    every seat in contact 10.8 to 12.0 s after
#   4 more only after 15 to 40 s               being told (widened at +2.6 to 2.9 s, by 3
#   4 to 20 attempts carried by nothing        own relays each), 2 attempts by nothing
#   4 seats reaped it (6 reaps), the far 2   no reap at either end
#   2 seats given back (D-060)               nobody given back
#
# which is the shape of runs/split201156-9 to the line. The booked prediction held in all
# three runs with the patch and failed in the control it was built to fail in. What the
# two runs do NOT separate is (a) from (b): with both announced relays dead the road
# found is a relay the two ends share, which needs (b) at one end and an awake relay at
# the other. And what no run here shows is that the natural failure was this and nothing
# else: one seat of that run matched the knob's state exactly, three others' attempts left
# by a relay and were never answered. S1-AA stays owed a census of plain evening runs.
#
# Vendored tree patched in place. Re-apply on a toxcore bump with
#   git apply patches/0040-*.patch
# and check tools/build-tox.ps1's markers for 0040 in TCP_connection.c and group_chats.c.
diff --git a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/TCP_connection.c b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/TCP_connection.c
index 84df5f3..2515d0c 100644
--- a/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/TCP_connection.c
+++ b/vendor/c-toxcore/toxcore/TCP_connection.c
@@ -58,6 +58,9 @@ struct TCP_Connections {
     bool onion_status;
     uint16_t onion_num_conns;
 
+    /* p2p-poker (patch 0040): a group's instance keeps its relays awake. */
+    bool p2p_poker_relays_stay_awake;
+
     /* Network profile for all TCP client packets. */
     Net_Profile *_Nullable net_profile;
 };
@@ -1625,6 +1628,53 @@ int add_tcp_number_relay_connection(const TCP_Connections *tcp_c, int connection
     return 0;
 }
 
+void p2p_poker_tcp_relays_stay_awake(TCP_Connections *tcp_c)
+{
+    tcp_c->p2p_poker_relays_stay_awake = true;
+}
+
+/* p2p-poker (patch 0040): a peer that one named relay does not reach is also looked
+ * for on the relays this client is connected to already.
+ *
+ * Both ends draw their relays from the same short public list, so they usually
+ * share one and do not know which. A routing request costs one small packet a
+ * relay; where the peer is connected the relay carries an out-of-band packet to it
+ * at once, and says ONLINE as soon as the peer asks for this client too -- which it
+ * does, running the same code. */
+uint32_t p2p_poker_register_on_own_relays(const TCP_Connections *tcp_c, int connections_number, uint32_t leave_free)
+{
+    const TCP_Connection_to *con_to = get_connection(tcp_c, connections_number);
+
+    if (con_to == nullptr) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    uint32_t used = 0;
+
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < MAX_FRIEND_TCP_CONNECTIONS; ++i) {
+        if (con_to->connections[i].tcp_connection != 0) {
+            ++used;
+        }
+    }
+
+    uint32_t added = 0;
+
+    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < tcp_c->tcp_connections_length && used + leave_free < MAX_FRIEND_TCP_CONNECTIONS; ++i) {
+        const TCP_con *tcp_con = get_tcp_connection(tcp_c, i);
+
+        if (tcp_con == nullptr || tcp_con->status != TCP_CONN_CONNECTED) {
+            continue;
+        }
+
+        if (add_tcp_number_relay_connection(tcp_c, connections_number, i) == 0) {
+            ++used;
+            ++added;
+        }
+    }
+
+    return added;
+}
+
 /** @brief Add a TCP relay tied to a connection.
  *
  * This should be called with the same relay by two peers who want to create a TCP connection with each other.
@@ -1909,8 +1959,32 @@ static void do_tcp_conns(const Logger *_Nonnull logger, TCP_Connections *_Nonnul
                 tcp_relay_on_online(tcp_c, i);
             }
 
+            /* p2p-poker (patch 0040): and never in a group's own instance.
+             *
+             * A relay goes to sleep when every connection that locks it is asleep,
+             * and a connection is asleep while its peer is direct. That is a
+             * messenger's economy: two friends who reach each other directly need
+             * no relay between them. A group is not two friends. A member that can
+             * be reached through relays alone is told of every other member by ONE
+             * relay of that member's (the announce has room for one), and sends its
+             * handshake request there out of band -- which the relay delivers only
+             * to a client that is CONNECTED to it. A seat whose peers were all
+             * direct had put every relay it has to sleep, that one included, some
+             * thirty seconds after joining: its address in the group was a closed
+             * door, and it stayed closed until something else woke the relay.
+             *
+             * Measured, runs/split201156-9 (2026-09-19, eight seats on one machine,
+             * the ninth far and relay-only): every local seat slept its relays
+             * between 27 and 38 s; the far seat, whose sync with the founder was
+             * slow that evening, was made known to them between 38 and 84 s; four
+             * of the eight never shook hands with it in 120 s and five reaped its
+             * entry (attempts 10, last packet 30 s ago -- the falsifier patch 0029
+             * booked for itself). In the runs where the far seat was known BEFORE
+             * the relays slept, nothing was reaped. The cost of staying awake is
+             * the handful of idle relay connections a table has anyway. */
             /* p2p-poker (patch 0027): and it carries no awake peer's out-of-band path. */
             if (tcp_con->status == TCP_CONN_CONNECTED
+                    && !tcp_c->p2p_poker_relays_stay_awake
                     && !tcp_con->onion && tcp_con->lock_count > 0
                     && tcp_con->lock_count == tcp_con->sleep_count
                     && !p2p_poker_relay_carries_awake_oob(tcp_c, i)
